-/*
- * /proc/ctrs/fsnotify: the ctrs guest agent reports changes made on the
- * host side of a virtiofs share, which the guest's VFS never sees. Each
- * write is one "<kind> <absolute path>" line, kind m (modified), c
- * (created) or r (removed). The path resolves in the writer's mount
- * namespace. Stale dcache entries are dropped so lookups see the host's
- * state, then the matching fsnotify event is raised on the parent and
- * child. FSEvents flags are sticky, so a "created" report for a name whose
- * inode is unchanged is raised as a modification. A file whose name now
- * holds another inode, or is gone, gets the link-count and delete-self
- * events a local unlink raises, so per-file watchers re-watch the name.
- * Files are never written. Mountpoints and directories are not
- * invalidated, since that would detach mounts under them.
- *
- * The host also reports the changes this guest made itself. An event is
- * skipped only when it provably reports the guest's own change: a removed
- * name that is negative here, or a name whose inode this guest changed and
- * whose host lookup returns the same node with the cached size, mtime and
- * ctime. Any other same-node change is raised in place without dropping
- * the dentry. Kind s (rescan) reports that the host lost events under a
- * directory: every cached dentry of the share is revalidated against the
- * host before its next use.
+/* /proc/ctrs/fsnotify accepts privileged host change reports. Rescans
+ * revalidate virtiofs caches and recover watched directory changes; an
+ * exhausted name set reports queue overflow to affected inotify groups.
  */
